courses.learning_outcomes:
Field studies allow students to understand real-world situations and supplement the knowledge from lectures. After taking the course, students will be able to:

• collect, analyse, and categorise field information by observation, interviewing, survey, and other applicable methods;

• assess and interpret social, economic, and environmental knowledge of the studied areas;

• compare and contrast geographical features across different countries or regions;

• summarise and evaluate the opportunities and challenges facing people, the environment, and society;

• develop a holistic understanding in a tempo-spatial context.

On successful completion of this course, the student will be able to:

• outline key ideas used by geographers in understanding natural resource management issues;
• understand how natural processes are influencing natural resource management;
• acquire knowledge related to physical earth resource, biotic and ecological resource and socio-cultural aspects of natural resource management;
• develop an understanding and skills necessary for management of natural and man-made resources;
• enhance research skills, with a focus on problem solving.

